IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `itaa-super`

-- Income Tax Assessment Act 1997, ss 290-230, 290-235, 292-85, 292-102, 294-35, 301-10, 302-60, 302-145, 302-195 -- worked cases.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Nothing here has been checked
-- against a decided case or subsidiary legislation (none was retrieved).
--
-- Fixtures are generated from a table of which flags are set (scratchpad gen_income-tax-assessment-act-1997.py). Expected results were written by
-- hand from the text.

`Chloe's downsizer contribution (c094)` MEANS Downsizer WITH
    `age when contributing` IS 67
    `main residence exemption applied at least partly` IS TRUE
    `years held by the couple before the disposal` IS 30
    `dwelling in Australia and not mobile` IS TRUE
    `days after the change of ownership` IS 30
    `chosen` IS TRUE
    `earlier downsizer from another dwelling` IS FALSE
    `amount contributed` IS 300000
    `your earlier downsizer contributions` IS 0
    `capital proceeds of both interests` IS 1500000
    `other downsizer contributions from these proceeds` IS 0

`Liam's downsizer contribution (c094)` MEANS Downsizer WITH
    `age when contributing` IS 70
    `main residence exemption applied at least partly` IS TRUE
    `years held by the couple before the disposal` IS 30
    `dwelling in Australia and not mobile` IS TRUE
    `days after the change of ownership` IS 30
    `chosen` IS TRUE
    `earlier downsizer from another dwelling` IS FALSE
    `amount contributed` IS 300000
    `your earlier downsizer contributions` IS 0
    `capital proceeds of both interests` IS 1500000
    `other downsizer contributions from these proceeds` IS 300000

§ `ss 290-230, 290-235`

-- c057: Liam's low-income year (his income is not stated; hypothetical). 30,000 -> 18% of 3,000 = 540; 38,000 -> 18% of
-- 2,000 = 360; 40,000 is not less than $40,000 -> nil.
#ASSERT `the spouse contribution offset` 30000 3000 TRUE FALSE EQUALS 540
#ASSERT `the spouse contribution offset` 38000 3000 TRUE FALSE EQUALS 360
#ASSERT `the spouse contribution offset` 40000 3000 TRUE FALSE EQUALS 0

§ `s 292-85(2)`

-- k089: Jake's $100,000. The concessional cap is an indexed INPUT; at the 2017-18 base of $25,000 the cap is 4 x 25,000 =
-- 100,000, so the contribution is within it at any cap of at least that. Nil if the total balance has reached the general cap.
#ASSERT `the non-concessional contributions cap` 25000 500000 2000000 EQUALS 100000
#ASSERT `the non-concessional contributions cap` 30000 2000000 2000000 EQUALS 0

§ `s 292-102`

-- c094: $300,000 each. Liam's ceiling is the lesser of 300,000 and 1,500,000 less Chloe's 300,000.
#ASSERT `the amount covered as a downsizer contribution` `Chloe's downsizer contribution (c094)` EQUALS 300000
#ASSERT `the amount covered as a downsizer contribution` `Liam's downsizer contribution (c094)` EQUALS 300000

§ `s 294-35`

#ASSERT `the transfer balance cap on first having an account` 2017 0 EQUALS 1600000

§ `ss 301-10, 301-20`

-- c092: Chloe's pension at 63 -- nothing assessable. Under 60, the taxable component is assessable (offsets not encoded).
#ASSERT `the assessable part of a member benefit from a taxed fund` 63 400000 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the assessable part of a member benefit from a taxed fund` 58 400000 EQUALS 400000

§ `ss 302-60, 302-145, 302-195`

-- c087 (Sue), c096 (Chloe), d093 (Priya): a spouse is a dependant. c098 (Oliver 58, Ella 55) and k098 (Ruby 46): an adult
-- child is not, unless dependent or interdependent. A hypothetical taxable component of 300,000.
#ASSERT `the recipient is a death benefits dependant` `the spouse or former spouse` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `the recipient is a death benefits dependant` `an adult child` FALSE

#ASSERT `the assessable part of a death benefit lump sum` `the spouse or former spouse` FALSE 300000 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the assessable part of a death benefit lump sum` `an adult child` FALSE 300000 EQUALS 300000

#ASSERT `the maximum rate on a non-dependant's taxable component in percent` FALSE EQUALS 15

